WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.600
SPEAKER: To get started with
the Cloud Natural Language API

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.480
for Python, you need
a GCP project which

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.830
you can set up in the console.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.090
In your project, enable
the Google Natural Language

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.410
API for that project.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.980
Then create a service account
and download the private key

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.110
as JSON.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.800
You use these credentials
to access Google Cloud APIs.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.740
So keep it secure and out of
your code and public repos.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.240
To access the credentials
from your project,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.880
set up an environment variable.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.310
I'm going to use
PyCharm as my ID.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.900
So the way I set up a
variable is different.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.060
I'll show you that shortly.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.540
If you aren't using
PyCharm, go to the terminal

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.210
and set an environment variable
to the path of your service

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:04.830
account.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:07.500
Prepare your environment
for Python development.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.750
Again, I'm using
PyCharm, but you

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.040
can use whatever dev
environment you prefer.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.610
If you are using
PyCharm, then under Run,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.040
select Edit Configurations.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.710
In the section
environment, there

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.330
is an Environment
Variable field.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.660
Click on this and add the Google
application credentials here.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:35.240
Next, install the client library
from the terminal, or IDE.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.470
If you use Cloud
Shell in the console,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.040
you can skip this step since
the required dependencies

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.080
are already included.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.570
Make a new file or
open an existing file.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.450
Import the Google
Cloud Client Library

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.190
and then instantiate a client.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.220
Provide some text to analyze.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.000
I'm going to go with a
classic example here.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.270
Now, create a document
which has a field called

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.240
content set to the
text you are analyzing

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.430
and a field code type
which, as the name suggests,

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:14.830
is the type of text--

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.170
in this case, plain text.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.650
Call analyze_sentiment,
passing the document.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.030
This function returns an
analyze_sentiment response.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.410
analyze_sentiment response has
three properties-- document

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:33.020
sentiment, the overall
sentiment of the input document;

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.330
language, the
language of the text;

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.480
and sentences, the sentiment
for all the sentences

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.660
in the document.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.630
Set the sentiment variable
to the document_sentiment.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:51.020
Log the parameters of
sentiment score and magnitude.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.590
Score is a sentiment
score between negative 1

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.720
for negative sentiment
and positive 1

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.240
for positive sentiment.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.130
Magnitude is a number
between 0 and infinity,

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.070
which represents the
absolute magnitude

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.750
of the sentiment regardless
of positive or negative score.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.700
OK, go ahead and run the code.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.470
And there you go.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.060
You've just sent
your first request

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.587
to the Natural Language API.

00:03:17.587 --> 00:03:19.920
But there's so much more that
the Cloud Natural Language

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.200
API can do, like analyzing
syntax and annotating text.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.630
To find out more about
the client libraries,

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.600
consult natural language basics
or work through the sentiment

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.900
analysis tutorial and check
out the Cloud documentation.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

